          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            While areas of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+topology"} general topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            have easily identifiable categories of main objects of study (e.g., categories of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            is not traditionally associated with a prominent, easily identifiable category.
          %p
            For example, in Fremlin’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Measure+Theory"} Measure Theory
            one reads (§234):
          %blockquote
            %p One of the striking features of measure theory, compared with other comparably abstract branches of pure mathematics, is the relative unimportance of any notion of ‘morphism’. The theory of groups, for instance, is dominated by the concept of ‘homomorphism’, and general topology gives a similar place to ‘continuous function’. In my view, the nearest equivalent in measure theory is the idea of ‘inverse-measure-preserving function’ (234A).
          %p
            This article reviews existing categories in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+analysis"} real analysis
            %strong compact strictly localizable enhanced measurable spaces
            enjoys excellent categorical properties and matches the existing practice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+analysis"} real analysis
            closely.
          %p
            This article concentrates on measure theory as it is used in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+analysis"} real analysis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistics"} statistics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+processes"} stochastic processes
            and other areas of analysis. In particular, given the existing practice in these fields, we take it for granted that we must identify functions that are equal almost everywhere. Other areas, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descriptive+set+theory"} descriptive set theory
          %p
            We also remark that the use of enhanced measurable spaces instead of measure spaces is for strictly esthetic reasons (like avoiding making noncanonical choices of measures), and everything works equally well with traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+spaces"} measure spaces
            instead.
          %h2#elementary_constructions Elementary constructions
          %h3#measurable_spaces Measurable spaces
          %p
            Recall that a
            %strong measurable space
            is a pair
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-algebra"} σ-algebra
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            closed under complements and countable unions. Elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            are known as
            %strong measurable subsets
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Morphisms of measurable spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon(X,M)\to(X',M')
            are known as
            = succeed "." do
              %strong measurable maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X\to X'
            that reflect measurable sets, i.e., if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'\in M'
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}m'\in M
          %p
            The resulting
            %strong category of measurable spaces
            does not identify measurable maps that are equal almost everywhere, so cannot be used directly in real analysis and related fields. This is not merely an inconvenience: many important theorems in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            are false unless such an identification is made. For example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%5Ep-spaces"} L^p-spaces
            would cease to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+spaces"} normed spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+theorem"} Radon–Nikodym theorem
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riesz+representation+theorem"} Riesz representation theorem
            would fail too.
          %h3#measure_spaces Measure spaces
          %p
            Having recognized the need to identify measurable maps that are equal almost everywhere, we can see that the data of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            is insufficient to perform such an identification, since there is no way of knowing which measurable sets have measure 0.
          %p
            A
            %strong measure space
            is a triple
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,\mu)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu\colon M\to[0,\infty]
            that sends countable disjoint unions of sets to the sum of corresponding values.
          %p Two measurable maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon(X,M,\mu)\to(X',M',\mu')
          %p
            are
            %strong equal almost everywhere
            if the set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f\ne g]=\{x\in X\mid f(x)\ne g(x)\}
          %p
            is contained in some set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in N_\mu
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\mu=\{m\in M\mid \mu(m)=0\}
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-ideal"} σ-ideal
            of measurable sets with
            = succeed "-measure" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                \-negligible sets
          %h3#too_much_data_required Too much data required
          %p Requiring a measurable space to be equipped with a specific choice of a measure forces us to make noncanonical choices that may be unnecessary.
          %p
            Many constructions and theorems in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            do not require a specific choice of a measure but merely require us to know which measurable sets have measure 0. Examples include the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riesz+representation+theorem"} Riesz representation theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+theorem"} Radon–Nikodym theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%5Ep-spaces"} L^p-spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1(X,M,\mu)
            is canonically isomorphic to the vector space of finite measures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M)
            that vanish on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\mu
            via the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+theorem"} Radon–Nikodym theorem
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/p
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%5Ep-spaces"} L^p-spaces
          %p
            Often, we do have a canonical choice of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-ideal"} σ-ideal
            of negligible sets even though we do not have a canonical measure.
          %p
            For example, a subset of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            is negligible if its preimage in any chart is a Lebesgue-negligible subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}^n
          %p
            Likewise, a subset of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+group"} locally compact group
            is negligible if it is negligible with respect to some (hence all) nonzero left-invariant or right-invariant measure.
          %h3#enhanced_measurable_spaces Enhanced measurable spaces
          %p
            The above considerations lead us to the notion of an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+measurable+space"} enhanced measurable space
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong enhanced measurable space
              is a triple
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N\subset M
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-ideal"} σ-ideal
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              whose elements are known as
              = succeed "." do
                %strong negligible sets
          %p We can keep the same definition of a measurable map. Two measurable maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon(X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
          %p
            are
            %strong equal almost everywhere
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f\ne g]
          %p
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong subnegligible set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,\mu)
            yields an enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N_\mu)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\mu
            as above.
          %p
            A map of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+spaces"} measure spaces
            is measurable if and only if it is measurable as a map of enhanced measurable spaces and two maps of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+spaces"} measure spaces
            are equal almost everywhere if and only if they are equal almost everywhere as maps of enhanced measurable spaces.
          %p
            An enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            is
            %strong complete
            if subnegligible sets are negligible, i.e., the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-ideal"} σ-ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is closed under passage to arbitrary subsets. This definition generalizes the usual notion of completeness of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+spaces"} measure spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            has a
            %strong completion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\hat M,\hat N)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat N
            is the σ-ideal of subnegligible sets and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat M
            is the σ-algebra whose elements are symmetric differences of subnegligible and measurable sets.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Everything in this article could be done with conventional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+spaces"} measure spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,\mu)
              instead of enhanced measurable spaces. Enhanced measurable spaces are only introduced to enhance the clarity of exposition and avoid making noncanonical choices of measures in some constructions. Also, separating
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
              from their underlying spaces makes the statements of some theorems cleaner, e.g., the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+theorem"} Radon–Nikodym theorem
              now says that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+module"} free module
              of rank one over measurable functions.
          %h3#measures_on_enhanced_measurable_spaces Measures on enhanced measurable spaces
          %p
            A
            %strong measure
            on an enhanced measurable space is a measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            on the measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M)
            that vanishes on every element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is
            %strong faithful
            if for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in M
            the relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(m)=0
            implies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is
            %strong semifinite
            if for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in M
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(m)=\infty
            there is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'\in M
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'\subset m
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\lt\mu(m')\lt\infty
          %p Typically, we are only interested in enhanced measurable spaces that admit a faithful semifinite measure, given that having a decent supply of measures is a prerequisite for developing measure theory. This property follows automatically from the strict localizability property introduced below.
          %p
            An enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            is
            %strong σ-finite
            if it admits a faithful finite measure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu\colon M\to[0,1]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in M
            is
            %strong σ-finite
            if the induced enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∩
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ∩
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (m,M\cap 2^m,N\cap 2^m)
            is σ-finite.
          %p
            A subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subset X
            is
            %strong σ-measurable
            if for every σ-finite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in M
            we have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\cap m\in M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subset X
            is
            %strong σ-negligible
            (respectively
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong σ-subnegligible
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in M
            we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\cap m\in N
            (respectively
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\cap m
            is subnegligible). An enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            is
            %strong locally determined
            if every σ-measurable subset is measurable and every σ-negligible subset is negligible. Every σ-finite enhanced measurable space is locally determined.
          %p
            By
            %a{:href => "#Fremlin"} Fremlin
            (§213D), for every enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            we can construct a complete locally determined enhanced measurable space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tilde M,\tilde N)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde M
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde N
            are the sets of σ-measurable and σ-negligible subsets, respectively, for the completion
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\hat M,\hat N)
          %p
            In particular,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\subset \tilde M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mover
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N\subset \tilde N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is measurable, inducing a morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tilde M,\tilde N)\to(X,M,N)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mover
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\subset \tilde M
            can be proper. One reasonable redefinition of a measurable map of enhanced measurable spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1,M_1,N_1)\to(X_2,M_2,N_2)
            that need not be complete or locally determined is a map of sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X_1\to X_2
            that reflects symmetric differences of σ-subnegligible and σ-measurable sets. Under such a definition, the above map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M',N')\to(X,M,N)
            is an isomorphism, with its inverse again given by the identity map of sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to X
          %p It is for this reason that it is safe to assume enhanced measurable spaces to be complete and/or locally determined from the start. Every strictly localizable enhanced measurable space is automatically locally determined, so there is no need for us to separately impose local determinedness.
          %h3#precomposition_does_not_respect_equality_almost_everywhere Precomposition does not respect equality almost everywhere
          %p
            If we now attempt to construct a category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+spaces"} measure spaces
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+measurable+spaces"} enhanced measurable spaces
          %p To see this, suppose
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon(X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
          %p
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+maps"} measurable maps
            that are equal almost everywhere.
          %p Suppose that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ″
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ″
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ″
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon(X'',M'',N'')\to(X,M,N)
          %p
            is a measurable map. If composition is to be compatible with equality almost everywhere, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ h
            would have to be equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\circ h
            almost everywhere. However,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi h
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f\circ h\ne g\circ h]=h^{-1}[f\ne g].
          %p
            Although
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f\ne g]
            is subnegligible, there is absolutely no reason why its preimage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^{-1}[f\ne g]
            should be subnegligible.
          %p
            Indeed, it is quite easy to construct examples where this is false: the inclusion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+set"} Cantor set
            into real numbers is a Borel measurable map. If we equip real numbers with the Lebesgue measure and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+set"} Cantor set
            with the product measure, then the inclusion map does not reflect subnegligible sets.
          %p Thus, making the equivalence relation compatible with precomposition naturally leads us to an additional restriction on measurable maps.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p A measurable map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon(X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
            %p
              %strong reflects negligible sets
              if for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n'\in N'
              the preimage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi n
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}n'
              is negligible. That is to say, the preimage map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}
              preserves negligible sets.
          %p By inspection, precomposition with a measurable map that reflects negligible sets preserves both equivalence relations: equality almost everywhere and weak equality almost everywhere (defined below).
          %p A measurable map reflects subnegligible sets if and only if it reflects negligible sets.
          %p
            If
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N'=\{\emptyset\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            reflects negligible sets. In particular, taking
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi R
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Borel
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X',M',N')=(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{R}_{\mathsf{Borel}},\{\emptyset\})
          %h3#negligibility_reflection Negligibility reflection
          %p Another way to look at the negligibility reflection condition introduced in the previous section is as follows.
          %p
            Consider an enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in N
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M=N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            should be indistinguishable from the empty set. Thus, the inclusion map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\emptyset,\{\emptyset\},\{\emptyset\})\to(X,M,N)
            should be an isomorphism in whatever category we are constructing.
          %p
            Therefore, it is reasonable to expect that there are no morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1,M_1,N_1)\to(X,M,N)\cong(\emptyset,\{\emptyset\},\{\emptyset\})
            unless
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1\in N_1
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ″
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ″
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ″
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon(X',M',N')\to(X'',M'',N'')
            is a measurable map and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo ″
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n''\in N''
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to a map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi n
                    %mo ″
                    %mo →
                    %mi n
                    %mo ″
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}n''\to n''
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n''
            is negligible, we expect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi n
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}n''
            to be negligible. This leads us once again to the negligibility reflection condition.
          %h2#postcomposition_respects_equality_almost_everywhere Postcomposition respects equality almost everywhere.
          %p Suppose
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon(X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
          %p
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+maps"} measurable maps
            that are equal almost everywhere. Suppose that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ″
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ″
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ″
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon(X',M',N')\to(X'',M'',N'')
          %p is a measurable map. We have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [h\circ f\ne h\circ g]\subset [f\ne g].
          %p
            Since the right side is subnegligible, so is the left side. Therefore,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\circ f
            is equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\circ g
            almost everywhere.
          %p Thus, we get a category of enhanced measurable spaces with morphisms being equivalence classes of measurable maps that reflect subnegligible sets modulo equality almost everywhere. This is not quite the category we need, as explained in the next section.
          %h3#weak_equality_almost_everywhere Weak equality almost everywhere
          %p Suppose
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon(X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
          %p
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+maps"} measurable maps
            that are equal almost everywhere. Suppose that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon(X',M',N')\to(\{0,1\},2^{\{0,1\}},\{\emptyset\})
          %p
            is a measurable map. Such measurable maps are in bijection with elements of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi h
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^{-1}\{1\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'\in M'
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+function"} characteristic function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{m'}
          %p We have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [h\circ f\ne h\circ g]=f^{-1}m'\oplus g^{-1}m',
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'=h^{-1}\{1\}
            is as above. Since
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}m'\oplus g^{-1}m' \subset [f\ne g],
          %p
            the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}m'\oplus g^{-1}m'
            is subnegligible.
          %p
            The condition stating that the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}m'\oplus g^{-1}m'
            is subnegligible for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'\in M'
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            are the same almost everywhere.
          %p
            It is quite natural to ask whether the converse holds. For example, two continuous maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon X\to X'
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T_0"} T_0-topological spaces
            are equal if and only if for every open subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'\subset X'
            we have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}U'=g^{-1}U'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'\in M'
            the symmetric difference
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}m'\oplus g^{-1}m'
            is subnegligible.
          %p
            However,
            %a{:href => "#Fremlin"} Fremlin
            (Example 343I) constructs an example of a measurable negligibility-reflecting map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:(X,M,\mu)\to(X,M,\mu)
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi 𝔠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,\mu)=\{0,1\}^{\mathfrak{c}}
            is a product of continuum many discrete measurable spaces
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            we have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)\ne x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi m
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}m\oplus m
            is negligible. Thus, the identity map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
            is not equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            almost everywhere and yet,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            have the same preimage maps, up to subnegligible sets.
          %p Such a situation is pathological, and is it turns out, it is the condition of having the same preimages up to subnegligible sets that produces nonpathological theory when the two conditions differ (they do coincide for many spaces, as we will see below).
          %p These considerations naturally lead us to a coarser equivalence relation.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p Two measurable maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon(X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
            %p
              are
              %strong weakly equal almost everywhere
              if for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m'\in M'
              the set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}m'\oplus g^{-1}m'
            %p is a subnegligible set.
          %p
            Equality almost everywhere implies weak equality almost everywhere. The converse is true if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X',M',N')
            is
            %strong countably separated
            (Fremlin, Proposition 343F), i.e., there is a countable subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ″
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M''\subset M'
            that covers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
            and for any two points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
            there is an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M''
            that contains exactly one of them.
          %p An enhanced measurable space is countably separated if and only if it admits an injective measurable map into real numbers. Most measurable spaces (other than some counterexamples) in an introductory real analysis book are countably separated, which explains why the finer equivalence relation of equality almost everywhere is sufficient for many practical purposes.
          %h3#the_category_of_enhanced_measurable_spaces The category of enhanced measurable spaces
          %p We collect what we have learned so far.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Measurable spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M)
                do not have enough information: they lack the data of sets of measure 0.
            %li
              %p
                Measure spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,\mu)
                have too much information: they require us to make arbitrary unnecessary choices of measures
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %li
              %p
                Enhanced measurable spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
                have just the right amount of information.
            %li
              %p The condition of measurability of maps, i.e., reflection of measurable sets, must be strengthened to also require reflection of negligible sets, to ensure its compatibility with precomposition.
            %li
              %p The equivalence relation of equality almost everywhere must be coarsened to weak equality almost everywhere to ensure morphisms are fully determined by their preimages up to subnegligible sets.
            %li
              %p Optionally, if we want to incorporate enhanced measurable spaces that are not complete or locally determined, instead of requiring maps to reflect measurable and negligible sets, we must require them to reflect σ-subnegligible sets and symmetric differences of σ-subnegligible sets and σ-measurable sets. The resulting category is equivalent to the category of complete locally determined enhanced measurable spaces.
          %p Assembling these properties together, we arrive at the following definition, formulated in the complete version for simplicity. We remind the reader that later we restrict to strictly localizable spaces, which are automatically locally determined.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong category of enhanced measurable spaces
              is constructed as a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+category"} quotient category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a set,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-algebra"} σ-algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-ideal"} σ-ideal
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h2#localizability Localizability
          %p The category of enhanced measurable spaces captures the desired properties of measurable spaces and measurable maps as they are used in real analysis.
          %p
            To complete the construction, we must eliminate pathological objects that invalidate the main theorems of measure theory. In traditional textbooks on measure theory, this is accomplished by restricting to σ-finite measure spaces and/or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon+measures"} Radon measures
          %p
            We consider a simple generalization of σ-finiteness known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em strict localizability
            %em compactness
            (introduced by Marczewski; not to be confused with compactness of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %h3#localizability_as_a_necessary_prerequisite_for_measure_theory Localizability as a necessary prerequisite for measure theory
          %p We review some classic results due to Irving E. Segal and John L. Kelley. As explained in the next section, the localizability property is still not quite enough to eliminate all the pathologies. However, it does explain the Boolean algebra side of the story.
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              (Irving E. Segal, 1951; John L. Kelley, 1966.) The following properties are equivalent for an enhanced measurable space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
              that admits a faithful semifinite measure.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/N
                  is complete.
              %li
                %p The Lebesgue decomposition for σ-ideals holds.
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hahn+decomposition+theorem"} Hahn decomposition theorem
                  holds for signed measures.
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+theorem"} Radon–Nikodym theorem
                  holds.
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riesz+representation+theorem"} Riesz representation theorem
                  holds: the canonical map
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn 1
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\infty\to(L^1)^*
                  is an isomorphism.
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi M
                        %mo ,
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\infty(X,M,N)
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebra"} commutative von Neumann algebra
            %p
              A
              %strong localizable enhanced measurable space
              is an enhanced measurable space that admits a faithful semifinite measure and satisfies any of the above equivalent properties.
          %p
            The takeaway from this result is that without the localizability property much of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            as we know is false, so it is completely reasonable to require it from the start.
          %p We can also establish an analogue of the above theorem for morphisms.
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              (The relative Kelley–Segal theorem.) The following properties are equivalent for a morphism of localizable enhanced measurable spaces
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon(X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The induced homomorphism of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi M
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi N
                        %mo ′
                        %mo →
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}\colon M'/N'\to M/N
                  is complete, i.e., preserves suprema.
              %li
                %p
                  The map
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}
                  preserves the generator of σ-ideals provided by the Lebesgue decomposition.
              %li
                %p
                  The pushforward map
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
                  on measures preserves the Hahn decomposition.
              %li
                %p
                  The relative
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+theorem"} Radon–Nikodym theorem
                  holds:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi f
                            %mo *
                          %mi h
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mi μ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi h
                          %mo ⋅
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mo *
                          %mi μ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*(f^* h\cdot \mu)=h\cdot f_*\mu
              %li
                %p
                  The induced map
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi M
                        %mo ,
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mi M
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mi N
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*\colon L^1(X,M,N)\to L^1(X',M',N')
                  is a bounded linear map of Banach spaces.
              %li
                %p
                  The induced *-homomorphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%2A-algebras"} *-algebras
                  of bounded real-valued functions on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  is a normal *-homomorphism of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} commutative von Neumann algebras
            %p
              A
              %strong localizable map
              of enhanced measurable spaces is a morphism of enhanced measurable spaces that satisfies any of these equivalent properties.
          %p Again, the takeaway here is that we really want to only use localizable maps if we want to avoid a complete breakdown of measure theory as we know it.
          %p
            Surprisingly, once we impose conditions on objects (namely, strict localizability and compactness) that eliminate pathological objects, it turns out that
            %em all
            maps between such objects are localizable. This is a deep theorem essentially due to
            %a{:href => "#Fremlin"} Fremlin
            (implicitly contained in §451Q).
          %h3#enhanced_measurable_spaces_and_complete_boolean_algebras Enhanced measurable spaces and complete Boolean algebras
          %p In the previous section we constructed (essentially by definition) a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LEMS
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi CBAlg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LEMS \to CBAlg^{op}
          %p
            from the category of localizable enhanced measurable spaces and localizable maps to the opposite category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Boolean+algebras"} complete Boolean algebras
            and complete homomorphisms of Boolean algebras.
          %p
            This functor lands inside complete Boolean algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            that admit a faithful semifinite measure, i.e., a map of sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu\colon A\to[0,\infty]
            that is completely additive, vanishes only on 0, and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi sup
                    %msup
                      %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1=\sup \mu^{-1}[0,\infty)
            = succeed "." do
              %strong localizable Boolean algebras
          %p Thus, the above functor descends to a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LEMS
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi LBAlg
                    %mi op
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LEMS \to LBAlg^{op}.
          %p
            The opposite category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LBAlg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LBAlg
            is known as the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locales"} measurable locales
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
          %h3#stone_and_gelfand_dualities_for_localizable_boolean_algebras Stone and Gelfand dualities for localizable Boolean algebras
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            is an equivalence of categories from the opposite category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
            to the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+spaces"} Stone spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+locales"} Stone locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Boolean+algebras"} complete Boolean algebras
            and complete homomorphisms of Boolean algebras to the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stonean+spaces"} Stonean spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stonean+locales"} Stonean locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+spaces"} hyperstonean spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+locales"} hyperstonean locales
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Pavlov"} Pavlov
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
            is an equivalence of categories from the opposite category of (unital)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+C%2A-algebras"} commutative C*-algebras
            and (unital) *-homomorphisms and the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+spaces"} compact Hausdorff spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+regular+locales"} compact regular locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} commutative von Neumann algebras
            and (unital) normal *-homomorphisms to the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+spaces"} hyperstonean spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+locales"} hyperstonean locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebra"} commutative von Neumann algebra
            to the localizable Boolean algebra of its projections and a (unital) normal *-homomorphism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} commutative von Neumann algebras
            to the induced complete homomorphism of Boolean algebras of projections (
            %a{:href => "#Pavlov"}> Pavlov
            , Theorem 3.20).
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Pavlov"}> Pavlov
              , Propositions 2.72, 2.73; Theorem 3.20.) The following four categories are equivalent.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The opposite category of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} commutative von Neumann algebras
                  and unital normal *-homomorphisms.
              %li
                %p The opposite category of localizable Boolean algebras and complete homomorphisms.
              %li
                %p
                  The category of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+locales"} hyperstonean locales
                  and open maps.
              %li
                %p
                  The category of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+spaces"} hyperstonean spaces
                  and open maps.
            %p
          %p We take this theorem as a strong evidence that whatever good category of enhanced measurable spaces we end up constructing, it better be equivalent to the above four categories.
          %p We have already constructed a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LEMS
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi LBAlg
                    %mi op
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LEMS \to LBAlg^{op}.
          %p In the next section, we construct a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi LBAlg
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi LEMS
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LBAlg^{op}\to LEMS
          %p
            that exhibits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi LBAlg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LBAlg^{op}
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            (in particular, a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LEMS
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LEMS
          %h3#the_loomissikorski_construction The Loomis–Sikorski construction
          %p The functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi LBAlg
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi LEMS
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LBAlg^{op}\to LEMS
          %p is constructed as the composition of functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi LBAlg
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi HStonean
                  %mo →
                  %mi LEMS
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LBAlg^{op}\to HStonean\to LEMS,
          %p
            where the first functor is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+duality"} hyperstonean duality
            functor explained in the previous section.
          %p
            The second functor is known as the
            %strong Loomis–Sikorski construction
            (
            %a{:href => "#Loomis"}> Loomis
            , 1947;
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Sikorski"} Sikorski
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Pavlov"} Pavlov
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+topological+space"} hyperstonean topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,U)
            to the enhanced measurable space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-ideal"} σ-ideal
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nowhere+dense+sets"} nowhere dense sets
            (equivalently in the case of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstonean+spaces"} hyperstonean spaces
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meager+sets"} meager sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%83-algebra"} σ-algebra
            of subsets with the property of Baire, i.e.,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+differences"} symmetric differences
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+subsets"} clopen subsets
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nowhere+dense+sets"} nowhere dense sets
          %p
            The quotient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/N
            is easily seen to be precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Boolean+algebra"} complete Boolean algebra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+subsets"} clopen subsets
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and the latter is precisely the inverse of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+spectrum"} Stone spectrum
            functor evaluated on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi LBAlg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LBAlg^{op}
            as a retract of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LEMS
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LEMS
          %h3#summary Summary
          %p We have seen that in order to have a theory resembing conventional measure theory, we have to work with localizable enhanced measurable spaces and localizable morphisms.
          %p As it turns out, the essential image of the Loomis–Sikorski functor is the full subcategory of enhanced measurable spaces comprising precisely compact and strictly localizable enhanced measurable spaces, defined in the next section.
          %p In particular (and rather surprisingly), all morphisms of compact strictly localizable enhanced measurable spaces are automatically localizable, even though there are examples (one of which is given below) of nonlocalizable morphisms from a σ-finite enhanced measurable space to a completely atomic (i.e., discrete) enhanced measurable space, both of which are localizable.
          %p Thus, if one finds the necessity of extending the above equivalence of four categories to include some form of enhanced measurable spaces convincing enough, one can skip the next section and proceed directly to the summary. Below, we provide additional independent motivation for strict localizability and compactness that does not rely on the above reasoning.
          %h2#the_subtle_points_strict_localizability_and_compactness The subtle points: strict localizability and compactness
          %h3#strict_localizability Strict localizability
          %p
            We already established in the previous section that it is highly desirable (e.g., for Gelfand duality) to be able to lift complete homomorphisms of Boolean algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M'/N'\to M/N
            to morphisms of enhanced measurable spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)\to(X',M',N')
          %p
            Consider the simplest case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X',M',N')=(X',2^{X'},\{\emptyset\})
            is a discrete enhanced measurable space.
          %p
            Then complete homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{X'}\to M/N
            can be identified with
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i\in M/N
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in X'
            ) such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\ne j
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i p_j=0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi sup
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sup_i p_i=1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i
            form a partition of 1 in the complete Boolean algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/N
          %p
            Likewise, measurable maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)\to(X',2^{X'},\{\emptyset\})
            can be identified with
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i\in M
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\ne j
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i\cap P_j=\emptyset
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_i P_i=X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i
            form a (disjoint) partition of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Lifting complete homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{X'}\to M/N
            to morphisms of enhanced measurable spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)\to(X',2^{X'},\{\emptyset\})
            amounts to constructing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i
            such that the equivalence class of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/N
            equals
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i
          %p
            Pick some arbitrary representative
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_i
            for every
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\ne j
            we have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i p_j = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_i \cap Q_j \in N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the essential supremum of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i\oplus Q_i\in N
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_i
            are disjoint.
          %p Surprisingly, the answer is negative in general.
          %p
            An enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            is
            %strong strictly localizable
            if it is isomorphic to the coproduct of a set-indexed family of σ-finite enhanced measurable spaces.
          %p Coproducts of enhanced measurable spaces are computed in the expected manner:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi i
                    %mo )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i (X_i,M_i,N_i)=\left(\coprod_i X_i,\prod_i M_i,\prod_i N_i\right),
          %p
            i.e., a subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_i X_i
            is measurable or negligible if and only if its intersection with every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
            is measurable or negligible, respectively.
          %p
            A rather delicate example of a complete locally determined localizable measure space that is not strictly localizable can be found in
            %a{:href => "#Fremlin"} Fremlin
            (§216E).
          %p
            It is not difficult to see that a localizable enhanced measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,M,N)
            admits liftings of complete homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{X'}\to M/N
            if and only if it is strictly localizable. Indeed, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
